d  [Music]  guten tag from stilfred hello mr von  stilfried nice you could join us today  during the covid pandemic there has been  a drop in the amount of antibiotics  being prescribed a significant drop  could you explain why that might be  yes we've been seeing a decline in the  use of antibiotics in germany for years  now  germany is in any case in the bottom  third internationally and in europe when  it comes to using antibiotics  and has dropped even further during the  pandemics  does that mean that far too many were  prescribed in the past  of course in the last few years we've  become particularly conscious of the  fact that every single prescription is  potentially increasing the risk of  resistance  and of course it has something to do  with public awareness  if patients insist on receiving  antibiotics  they'll probably always be able to find  a doctor  my pleasure  in shanghai there were reports of people  screaming out of their windows at night  because they couldn't get enough to eat  this film that can no longer be seen in  china because beijing's online senses  deleted it shows a compilation of  telephone calls impressions and recorded  conversations in cordoned off districts  of shanghai  to combat the outbreak authorities are  instead administering herbal remedies  that are useless against covert 19 and  also harmful to health  still china is sticking to its strict  zero covert policy  shanghai has been in lockdown for a  month  it's not the first time traditional  chinese medicine or tcm has been used in  china's battle against covet 19 but its  distribution among people in shanghai  has been unprecedented  millions of batches of the homeopathic  capsule lianghor chinguin were handed  out to people under lockdown this month  zoe zhong says she and her four  flatmates received six boxes actually we  don't take it because  uh from the social media  that i followed it's not for like curing  your symptoms it's not preventing you  from the covet itself either lianghoa  chinguin's ingredients include  honeysuckle licorice root and other  herbs  tcm practitioners say it can help  suppress fever and sore throat but  health experts warn it's not an  alternative to vaccines  a leading chinese neurologist recently  hit out at the promotion of lianhua  chinguan he questioned why it received  delivery priority in shanghai when many  people under lockdown were struggling  with access to food we think that  the government should focus more on the  basic needs for the people  reports have also linked china's health  tsar zhong nanshan with the maker of the  pill healing pharmaceutical  john guides china's covet 19 policy as  head of the national health commission  he's said to have received funding from  e-link for his own medical foundation  and also signed a cooperation agreement  with the company  but beijing's also strongly backed the  tcm industry worth some 60 billion a  year according to the world health  organization the promotion of this this  kind of medical market is something  that's  certainly being encouraged the idea  being that if this  industry can be developed to a certain  level of maturity where uh  some very highly effective drugs are  developed  [Music]  then you know these these can become  part of the more mainstream mix of  different types of treatments not only  domestically but also internationally  the debate over the use of tcm has even  taken on a nationalistic tone people  criticizing it have been accused of  betraying china politics here seem to  once again be trumping scientific  efforts to beat covet 19.  the omicron variant is a problem for  china's zero covert strategy  in the spring hong kong had the world's  highest death rate among coronavirus  patients  now for the time being the wave seems to  have lost strength in the metropolis  [Music]  a hong kong travel agency struggles to  survive by turning itself into a grocery  instead of trips they sell food from  overseas  many others are shuttered because of the  pandemic border closure has made  traveling almost impossible for over two  years however the airport's departure  terminal is getting busier since the  beginning of this year hong kong's net  outflow has peaked at about 165  000 in april over five times the total  number of last year many are foreign  talents like john wood who left with his  business after eight years in hong kong  i want to be able to have my son where  he has the freedom to do simple things  like go to the beach or walk around  outdoors without a mask on and as a  global philanthropist i want to have the  freedom to travel around the world and  raise my seed funding and so for me hong  kong went from one of the most open  exciting cities in the world to being  this thing that is closed  and petrified where the government's  policies seem to change willy-nilly  the final straw is hong kong's adherence  to china's syracuse policy prompting the  toughest measures ever amid the omigran  outbreak not only individuals are voting  with their feet but also companies  companies are have significant problems  to refill positions to attract talent to  hong kong across industries it's even  reaching as far as financial services  industry which has usually been  perceived as the most resilient part of  it  in the latest survey released by the  european chamber of commerce about half  of the european companies in hong kong  plan to relocate in the coming year only  17 will firmly stay  hong kong is actually a popular base for  reaching the headquarters of foreign  businesses the exit of express comes on  top of the local brain drain triggered  by political upheavals and a sweeping  national security law when you wake up  every morning and see that journalists  are being arrested  um that  some newspaper publishers are being  arrested that there's no more freedom of  assembly no more freedom of speech that  hits me pretty hard you can't subject to  city state to all this solitude if 